## Why This Is Asked
Tests data governance skills: masking, policy registry, lineage, and performing under peak load in CNPA.

## Key Concepts
- Data masking across HTTP, Kafka, and DB
- Policy registry with versioning
- Data lineage and auditability
- Performance impact and verification under load

## Code Example
```javascript
function maskPII(value, policy) {
  // naive example: redact emails/phones per policy
  if (!value) return value;
  if (policy.hideEmails && /\\S+@\\S+/.test(value)) {
    return value.replace(/[^@]+@[^.]+\\.[^.]+/, 'REDACTED@domain');
  }
  if (policy.redactDigits) {
    return value.replace(/\\d/g, 'X');
  }
  return value;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the masking policy against evolving schemas without breaking dashboards?
- What metrics confirm compliant exposure levels under peak load?

## Why This Is Asked

Introduces feature flags and cache strategies with safe rollout and rollback, a practical skill for production CNPA systems.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and governance
- Read-through cache pattern with Redis
- Cache invalidation/write-through on updates
- Canary rollout and observable rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: flag check and cached read
function getEndpointData(endpoint){
  if (config.getFlag('cache:' + endpoint)){
    const v = cache.get(endpoint)
    if (v) return v
    const data = db.query(endpoint)
    cache.set(endpoint, data, TTL)
    return data
  }
  return db.query(endpoint)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test cache invalidation under concurrent writes?
- How would you handle a cache stampede or Redis outage?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical observability skills in a CNPA stack, ensuring candidates understand how to trace requests end-to-end rather than isolating individual components.

## Key Concepts

- Distributed tracing with OpenTelemetry across HTTP, database, Kafka, and Redis
- Trace propagation via W3C traceparent headers
- Sampling strategies that balance detail with performance overhead
- Backend visualization tools (Jaeger, Tempo) and troubleshooting workflows
- End-to-end verification under controlled test conditions

## Code Example

```javascript
// OpenTelemetry setup for Node.js HTTP API
const { NodeSDK } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-node');
const { HttpInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-http');
const { PgInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-pg');
const { KafkaInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-kafkajs');
const { RedisInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-ioredis');
const { JaegerExporter } = require('@opentelemetry/exporter-jaeger');
const { BatchSpanProcessor } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-base');

const sdk = new NodeSDK({
  serviceName: 'cnpa-api',
  spanProcessor: new BatchSpanProcessor(new JaegerExporter()),
  instrumentations: [
    new HttpInstrumentation(),
    new PgInstrumentation(),
    new KafkaInstrumentation(),
    new RedisInstrumentation(),
  ],
});

sdk.start();
```

This instrumentation automatically propagates W3C Trace Context headers across all components, ensuring end-to-end trace continuity.

## Why This Is Asked

Test practical tracing and end-to-end visibility across CNPA during retries and regional outages, ensuring the correlation persists across services and layers.

## Key Concepts

- Correlation IDs / distributed tracing across HTTP, DB, Kafka, Redis
- Middleware propagation, headers, and context propagation
- Retry semantics, idempotent writes, and rollback strategies
- Observability: logs, metrics, traces, dashboards

## Code Example

```javascript
// Express middleware to ensure correlation ID propagation
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const id = req.headers['x-correlation-id'] || uuidv4();
  req.correlationId = id;
  res.setHeader('X-Correlation-Id', id);
  next();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify the correlation ID is preserved through PostgreSQL writes and Kafka messages?
- How would you test this under retries and partial outages, including automated end-to-end tests?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-service consistency, patterns to prevent data drift, and failure scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- Outbox pattern, exactly-once semantics, idempotent sinks
- Reconciliation, deduplication, TTL tuning
- Observability and tests under outages

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: check idempotent sink
async function applyEvent(event){
  const key = `${event.entity}:${event.id}`;
  if(await redis.get(key)) return; // dedupe
  await redis.set(key, true, 'EX', TTL);
  // apply to cache
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test reconciliation under Kafka retention constraints?
- What metrics signal drift after recovery?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of cross-service state management, idempotence, and safe eventing in a CNPA stack at beginner level.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-service state storage (Redis vs Postgres)
- Idempotent producers and event schemas
- Sign-in flow with rate limiting and 429s
- End-to-end verification under load

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo JS: acquire lock, publish event, then proceed
async function attemptSignIn(userId){
  const locked = await redis.get(`lockout:${userId}`);
  if (locked) return {ok:false, code:429};
  // check policy in Postgres (omitted)
  // on failure:
  await pubClient.send({ key:`lockout:${userId}`, value: 'locked', ... });
  await redis.set(`lockout:${userId}`, 'LOCKED', 'EX', 900);
  return {ok:false, code:403};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test idempotence across retries?
- How would you evolve the schema if lockout duration varies per user?

## Why This Is Asked
Aimed to assess mastery of end-to-end guarantees across HTTP API, DB, Kafka, and Redis, including failure handling and read-model integrity.

## Key Concepts
- Outbox pattern
- Kafka transactional producers
- Idempotent consumers
- Cross-service failure handling
- Read model consistency and testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// PostgreSQL transaction: write user update and outbox row
BEGIN;
UPDATE users SET name = $1 WHERE id = $2;
INSERT INTO outbox (aggregate_type, aggregate_id, event_type, payload, sent)
VALUES ('user', $2, 'UserUpdated', '{id:' + $2 + ',name:' + $1 + '}', false);
COMMIT;
```

```javascript
// Pseudo publisher
async function publishOutbox() {
  const rows = await fetchUnsentOutbox();
  const txn = await kafka.beginTransaction();
  for (const r of rows) {
    await kafka.send({ topic: 'user.events', key: r.aggregate_id, value: r.payload }, txn);
  }
  await markOutboxSent(rows.map(r => r.id));
  await txn.commit();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement idempotent apply of events in Redis and read models?
- How would you simulate outages to ensure no duplicates or gaps?
